concern:
  heading: "Numbers, specs and people who aren't at a computer"
  body: >
    <p>Two objections, both practical. The first is accuracy: this industry runs on
    measurements, quantities, standards and prices, and a plausible-looking wrong number
    is worse than no number. We train the boundary hard — AI drafts the words around the
    numbers, a person owns the numbers themselves, and anything referencing a standard
    or a contract clause gets checked against the source. It is a drafting assistant,
    not an estimator.</p>
    <p>The second is that your people are on site, not at a desk. That's the actual
    design constraint of this version of the program: workflows that start from a voice
    note or a photo on a phone, because a workflow that requires sitting down at a
    laptop will not survive contact with a real week.</p>
faq:
  - q: "Will site staff actually use this?"
    a: "Only if it starts from how they already work, which is why we build around voice notes and phones rather than desktop tools. The honest answer is that adoption is better with supervisors and project managers than with everyone on the tools — and that's usually where the paperwork sits anyway."
  - q: "Can it price a job?"
    a: "No, and we teach people not to try. Pricing carries commercial risk and the tools will produce confident, wrong figures. It drafts the written parts of a quote or tender around numbers your estimator owns."
  - q: "Is it safe to put contract documents into it?"
    a: "With the right tool tier and terms, yes — and that setup is part of the training. Head contracts and commercially sensitive tender material need the business terms rather than a consumer account, and we cover which is which."
  - q: "We're a subcontractor with 20 staff, not a tier-one builder. Is this for us?"
    a: "Smaller businesses usually feel it more, because the person writing the variation is also the person running the job. The unwritten variation problem is a subcontractor problem far more than a head contractor one."
  - q: "What about drawings and models?"
    a: "Out of scope. This is training in the language and documentation layer — the writing, reading and summarising around the work. Design and modelling tools are a different purchase entirely."
